ing via brute force check or during sweep). Actually simplest: in the public API `find_intersections`, first validate: no vertical segments (raise), then detect any pair of segments whose supporting lines are collinear AND overlap → NotImplementedError. Three-segment concurrency: detect at a point where ≥3 segments pass. How to detect? During the sweep itself, if an event point has >2 segments involved, raise NotImplementedError. But careful: endpoint touching where an endpoint of seg A lies in interior of seg B is fine (that\'s 2 segments). If three segments meet at one point → raise.\n\nBut wait: random small-integer endpoints might produce 3-segment concurrence rarely; vertical segments never if we avoid same x for endpoints... Actually random endpoints could accidentally be vertical (same x). With coordinates 0..20 say, probability ~ 1/20 per segment. So generator must resample to avoid vertical segments. Also 3-segment concurrency is rare with small ranges but possible; the test instances need to be valid — regenerate/resample when invalid. Also collinear overlap: two segments on the same line overlapping — possible with small integer coords. Need to handle by rejection + resampling in tests, and NotImplementedError in library.\n\nHmm wait — what about two segments being collinear but NOT overlapping (touching at a single point, e.g., end-to-end collinear)? That\'s a single intersection point, fine to report. Only overlapping (infinite set) needs rejection. Actually even collinear touching at one point: e.g., A=(0,0)-(2,2), B=(2,2)-(4,4): intersect exactly at (2,2). That\'s fine. Collinear overlap: A=(0,0)-(4,4), B=(1,1)-(5,5): infinite. Reject that.\n\nAlso duplicate segments? Two identical segments = overlap → rejected by the collinear-overlap check. Good.\n\n**Brute force:** for each pair, compute intersection:\n- denominators d1 = cross(d1, d2) where d_i are direction vectors.\n- If d != 0: unique point, t = cross(p3-p1, d2)/d etc.; check t,s in [0,1]; return Fraction point.\n- If d == 0: collinear. Check overlap: project onto dominant axis; if intervals overlap in more than a point → infinite (return marker); if they touch at exactly one point → that point.\n\nFor comparing: brute force returns set of points plus info about degenerate overlaps. The main API will pre-validate and reject overlaps/concurrency before sweeping, so brute force used as oracle on validated inputs just returns finite point set. I\'ll implement `segment_intersection(p1,p2,p3,p4)` returning either None, a Fraction point, or raising/flagging "overlap".\n\nActually cleaner architecture:\n\ngeometry.py:\n- `orientation(a,b,c) -> int` (-1/0/1)\n- `Segment` maybe simple named tuple usage.\n- `line_intersection_point(p1,p2,p3,p4) -> Fraction point | None | OVERLAP sentinel`. Compute exact.\n- `segments_intersect(a, b) -> bool` for brute force? Better to return the point(s).\n- Validation helpers: `validate_no_vertical(segments)`, `find_collinear_overlap(segments)`.\n- `brute_force_intersections(segments) -> set[(Fraction,Fraction)]` raising NotImplementedError on overlap? Or returning special value. Let\'s have it raise ValueError(\'overlap\') internally and the test catches to resample. Hmm — better: `find_intersections` (public) validates everything up front and raises NotImplementedError for vertical / overlap / triple-concurrency. Triple concurrency detection requires finding all intersection points anyway — do it in the sweep: when processing an event, count how many distinct segments contain that point... Actually simpler: run brute-force-ish pairwise computation to collect all intersection points grouped by point; if any point has ≥3 segments through it → NotImplementedError. That\'s O(n²) validation which is acceptable and makes the sweep itself simpler (guarantee each event involves ≤2 segments). Document honestly that validation is O(n²) but the sweep proper is O((n+k) log n).\n\nWait but then the 400-segment case: does validation O(n²)=160k pair checks matter? Fine, fast.\n\nBut careful: is it cheating that find_intersections uses O(n²) validation? The task explicitly permits rejecting triple concurrency; detecting it robustly is easiest globally. Alternatively detect during sweep. Sweep-based detection is intricate. Given "document complexity honestly", I\'ll document: total algorithm O(n² + (n+k) log n) due to degeneracy validation; sweep core is O((n+k) log n).\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: detecting triple concurrency via pairwise intersections grouped by point: group all pairwise intersection points (as Fractions) into dict point→count of segments involved. For a point where m segments meet, number of pairwise hits is C(m,2). But a point could be endpoint-touch of 2 segments and interior crossing counted once each... Each pair contributes at most one point. Group by point: if a point appears from ≥2 distinct pairs... hmm, if 3 segments concur, there are 3 pairs all giving the same point. If 2 segments intersect at p, one pair gives p. But could two different pairs give the same point without triple concurrency? Pair (A,B) intersects at p, pair (C,D) intersects at p, but not all four through p? A∩B=p means A and B both contain p. C∩D=p means C,D contain p. So four segments contain p — that\'s ≥3 concurrent. So: any point produced by ≥2 pairs ⇒ ≥3 segments through p ⇒ NotImplementedError. And endpoint touches: A\'s endpoint on B\'s interior gives one pair-point p; if also A\'s endpoint equals B\'s endpoint etc., still one pair unless third segment. Good, clean rule: count pairs per point; if max≥2 → triple+ concurrency.\n\nCareful subtlety: T-shape where endpoint of A lies exactly at endpoint of B and also on C? Then pairs (A,B),(A,C),(B,C)? If B and C don\'t otherwise intersect at p... B contains its own endpoint p, C contains p? If p on C\'s interior then (B,C) pair: does B∩C include p? Yes p is on both B and C, so their intersection includes p (assuming not collinear overlap). So 3 pairs → detected. \n\nAnother subtlety: collinear touching segments: A=(0,0)-(2,0), B=(2,0)-(5,0) collinear touching at (2,0): one pair, point (2,0). Fine, reported. And overlap → rejected earlier.\n\nSo pipeline for `find_intersections(segments)`:\n1. Reject vertical segments.\n2. For all pairs: compute intersection status. Collect points dict point → set of segment ids containing it? Simpler: dict point → list of pairs. If len≥2 → NotImplementedError. If overlap → NotImplementedError.\n3. Run Bentley-Ottmann on the cleaned input; result set should equal brute force set (we can assert in debug/tests).\n\nNow the Bentley-Ottmann implementation. Standard:\n\nEvents: segment start (left endpoint), segment end (right endpoint), intersection points. Order events by (x, y). Use heapq with tuples (x: Fraction, y: Fraction, type, payload). Fraction comparisons are exact — good.\n\nStatus structure: active segments ordered by y at current sweep x. Since we reject verticals and use exact arithmetic, standard approach: status = sorted list keyed by y_at(x). Implementing with bisect against a comparator: use a key class or the "bisect with key via wrapper" trick. Python 3.10+ bisect supports `key=` parameter! bisect.bisect_left(a, x, key=f). That computes f(mid) each probe — fine. Key function: y-coordinate of segment at current sweep x. But sweep x changes; store current_x in a mutable holder or recompute keys lazily. Since bisect with key calls key(element) and compares to x\'s key... We\'d call bisect with x being a sentinel segment and key = lambda seg: y_of(seg, current_sweep_x). current_sweep_x stored in module-level/state variable updated before each bisect. That works and matches "a list with bisect against a comparator function is acceptable". I\'ll write my own bisect over comparator to be explicit, or use bisect with key. Using stdlib bisect with key= is cleanest; document complexity: insertions/deletions O(n) due to list, lookups O(log n) comparisons each O(1)-ish (Fraction arithmetic though—each y-at-x evaluation is O(log?) big-int ops; treat as O(1) word ops honestly? Just say O(log n) comparisons per operation, list insert/delete O(n), so worst case O(n² ) overall for the status structure — honest documentation).\n\nEvent handling (with no vertical segments, no triple points, but endpoint degeneracies allowed):\n\nAt event point p with x = p.x:\n1. Pop all events equal to p (same (x,y)) from queue.\n2. Determine segments ending at p, starting at p, and crossing at p (interior-interior crossing). With exact arithmetic and general-ish position (no triples), at most... crossing at interior point of both segments happens only at their mutual intersection; can\'t have more than one pair crossing at p (no triples). But p could be: \n   - start of A, end of B (endpoint touch)\n   - start of A, interior of B\n   - interior of A, end of B\n   - interior crossing of A and B (both interiors)\n   - end of both A and B\n   - start of both A and B\n   - start of A only, etc.\n\nStandard approach handling all cases: at event p:\n   - L = segments with right(endpoint at p... wait orientation) Let me define: left endpoint = smaller x (since non-vertical, x strictly increases along segment). Events: left endpoint, right endpoint, intersection.\n\n   At sweep position p:\n   - RL: segments for which p is the right endpoint.\n   - LR: segments for which p is the left endpoint.\n   - C: segments containing p in their interior (these are in the status structure, found by locate).\n\n   Remove RL ∪ C from status, swap order appropriately, reinsert RL∪LR... The classic: remove all segments ending or crossing at p; find neighbors pred/succ around removal gap; reinsert crossing + left-endpoint segments; check new adjacencies for intersections; also check adjacency between pred-of-gap and succ-of-gap after removal (only needed if nothing reinserted).\n\nSince no triple concurrency, at most 2 segments involved at p. That simplifies enormously! Cases:\n   -