eg_intersection(p,q,r,s)` → returns (\'none\',None) / (\'point\',(fx,fy)) / (\'overlap\',None). Used by validation & brute force & check_pair (check_pair can use \'point\').\n- `brute_force_intersections(segments)` → set of points; raises NotImplementedError on overlap? For oracle use, tests need valid inputs anyway. I\'ll have brute force return set and let find_intersections do validation separately calling seg_seg_intersection per pair.\n\nValidation in find_intersections:\n```\npts = {}  # point -> count of pairs\nfor i<j:\n    kind, pt = seg_seg_intersection(...)\n    if kind == \'overlap\': raise NotImplementedError(\'collinear overlap\')\n    if kind == \'point\': pts.setdefault(pt, []).append((i,j))\nfor pt, pairs in pts.items():\n    if len(pairs) >= 2: raise NotImplementedError(\'concurrent ≥3\')\n```\nThen sweep. Optionally assert sweep result == set(pts) — that\'s essentially free oracle built-in; but keep them separate (tests compare against independent brute force). Maybe debug assert off by default. Skip.\n\nComplexities documented: validation O(n² log n)-ish (Fraction gcd normalization), sweep O((n+k)·(n + log n · M)) where M bigint cost... document honestly: active list insert/delete O(n); each event does O(1) status mutations plus neighbor checks; total O(n log n) heap ops with Fraction keys; overall worst-case O(n² + k·n) plus log factors.\n\nTests (`tests/test_sweep.py`):\n- geometry unit tests: orientation signs, known intersections, fraction outputs, parallel-none, collinear touch, overlap detection.\n- rejection tests: vertical segment raises NotImplementedError; constructed triple-star raises NotImplementedError; collinear overlap raises NotImplementedError; degenerate zero-length ValueError.\n- handcrafted small scenarios with known answers: X crossing, T-touch, V-touch (endpoints), shared endpoints, collinear end-to-end touch.\n- Oracle: seeded RNG (random.Random(seed)), generate valid instance: sample segments with int endpoints in [0,25];