]*d2[0]\n    if denom != 0:\n        rx = p3[0]-p1[0]; ry = p3[1]-p1[1]\n        t_num = rx*d2[1]-ry*d2[0]\n        s_num = rx*d1[1]-ry*d1[0]\n        # careful signs: solve p1 + t d1 = p3 + s d2\n        # cross(p3-p1, d2) = t*denom ; cross(p3-p1, d1) = -s*denom? let me verify.\n```\n\nDerive: p1 + t d1 = p3 + s d2 → t d1 - s d2 = w where w = p3-p1. Cross with d2: t (d1×d2) = w×d2 → t = (w×d2)/denom. Cross with d1: -s (d2×d1) = w×d1 → s (d1×d2) = w×d1 → s = (w×d1)/denom. Good: t_num = w×d2, s_num = w×d1, denom=d1×d2.\n\nIn 2D, w×v = wx*vy - wy*vx.\n\nt,s ∈ [0,1] check with Fractions: t_num/denom between 0 and 1 inclusive: multiply by denom sign-aware. Use Fraction(t_num, denom) then 0<=t<=1. Fine.\n\nPoint: x = Fraction(p1x) + t*d1x etc. Since inputs ints, could compute as Fraction(t_num*p1x... just use Fractions.\n\nIf denom == 0: collinear lines. Check overlap vs disjoint/touch. Since collinear, project on axis with larger |d1| component. Segments [min,max] intervals along axis (using x if |d1x|>=|d1y| else y — d1 parallel d2 so same axis). Compute interval for each segment; if max(lo) < min(hi) → overlap → OVERLAP. If equal → single touch point → POINT. Else NONE.\n\nWait: also need actual collinearity (parallel but different lines): denom==0 means parallel; check orient(p1,p2,p3)==0 for collinear; if not → NONE (parallel distinct). Important!\n\nbrute_force:\n```python\ndef brute_force_intersections(segments):\n    out=set()\n    for i in range(len(segments)):\n        for j in range(i+1,len(segments)):\n            kind,pt = seg_seg_relation(*segs[i],*segs[j])\n            if kind==POINT: out.add(pt)\n            elif kind==OVERLAP: raise NotImplementedError(...)\n    return out\n```\nHmm brute force raising NotImplementedError on overlap: fine, documented.\n\nvalidate_input(segments): returns nothing, raises NotImplementedError(vertical), ValueError(degenerate), NotImplementedError(overlap), NotImplementedError(triple). Reuse pair loop collecting points→pairs.\n\nbentley_ottmann.py:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\nfrom .geometry import ...\n\nclass _Sweep:\n    def __init__(self, segments):  # segments normalized: left-to-right, tuple of int tuples\n        ...\n```\n\nPrecompute for each seg: lx,ly,dx,dy (ints), slope=Fraction(dy,dx), left pt, right pt.\n\ny_at(i,x): ly + Fraction(dy*(x-lx), dx) — with x Fraction. Return Fraction.\n\nkey(i) = (y_at(i, cur_x), slope[i]) where cur_x stored as list cell self._x (mutable attribute fine).\n\nStatus: plain list of seg indices kept sorted by key. Operations:\n- insort via manual bisect using key comparisons:\n```python\ndef _bisect(self, idx):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(self.active)\n    k = self.key(idx)\n    while lo<hi:\n        mid=(lo+hi)//2\n        if self.key(self.active[mid]) < k: lo=mid+1\n        else: hi=mid\n    return lo\n```\nDocument: O(log n) key evals, each O(1) Fraction mul (bigint); insert/delete into list O(n) memmove.\n\nHeap entries: (x, y, counter, payload_tuple). payload: (\'L\',i) / (\'R\',i) / (\'X\',i,j) with i<j.\n\nMain:\n\n```python\ndef find_intersections(segments):\n    segs = normalize+validate (raise as spec)\n    return _Sweep(segs).run()\n```\n\nrun():\n```python\nheap=[]; cnt=0\npush initial L events for each segment (sorted not needed)\nresults=set()\npending=set()\nactive=[]; active_set=set()\nself._x = None\nwhile heap:\n    x,y = heap[0][0], heap[0][1]\n    self._x = x\n    batch=[]\n    while heap and heap[0][0]==x and heap[0][1]==y:\n        batch.append(heapq.heappop(heap)[3])\n    Rset=set(); Lset=set(); Xpairs=[]\n    for item in batch:\n        if item[0]==\'L\': Lset.add(item[1])\n        elif item[0]==\'R\': Rset.add(item[1])\n        else:\n            i,j=item[1],item[2]\n            pending.discard(frozenset((i,j)))\n            if i in active_set and j in active_set: Xpairs.append((i,j))\n    cross = set()\n    for i,j in Xpairs: cross.add(i); cross.add(j); results.add((x,y))\n    # segments leaving: R ∪ cross ; continuing: cross - R... every segment in cross continues except if also R.\n    leaving = (Rset | cross)\n    cont = cross - Rset   # reinsert\n    entering = Lset\n    # find neighborhood BEFORE removal: positions of leaving members\n    # simpler: capture pred/succ around the whole leaving block:\n    # remove leaving\n    pred_succ = self._remove_block(leaving)  # returns (pred, succ) surrounding after removal\n    # insert entering + cont (sorted by key)\n    inserted = sorted(list(cont)+list(entering), key=self.key)  # careful: sorting with key uses cur_x fine\n    for idx in inserted: self._insert(idx)\n    # adjacency checks:\n    if inserted:\n        lo_idx = position of first inserted... after inserting all, the block spans [pos_first, pos_last]. Neighbors: element before block, elements within block consecutive pairs, element after block.\n```\n\nSimplify: gather all "involved" = leaving ∪ entering ∪ cross. Before ANY mutation, record for each involved member its current neighbors? Alternative simpler robust method: after performing removals+insertions, determine the affected index window and check all consecutive pairs within [win_lo-1, win_hi+1]. Implementation: track min/max positions touched.\n\nEasier concrete approach given small sets:\n- Before removal: find pos of any leaving member (they form contiguous block? R and cross members all have key-y == p.y at cur_x... yes! all segments through p have y_at(cur_x)==p.y hence contiguous in status (ties broken by slope but all in the tie group; could a non-through-p segment interleave? Its y_at == p.y would mean it contains p → triple, impossible. Contiguous ✓).\n- block_lo = min index over leaving, block_hi = max.\n- pred = active[block_lo-1] if block_lo>0; succ = active[block_hi+1] if block_hi+1<len.\n- Remove all leaving (by value).\n- Insert sorted inserted list sequentially.\n- After insertion, the inserted items are contiguous too (all tie at cur_x). Their positions: find via searching values (small set): pos_min = mi