urrent sweep x. Since we reject verticals and use exact arithmetic, standard approach: status = sorted list keyed by y_at(x). Implementing with bisect against a comparator: use a key class or the "bisect with key via wrapper" trick. Python 3.10+ bisect supports `key=` parameter! bisect.bisect_left(a, x, key=f). That computes f(mid) each probe — fine. Key function: y-coordinate of segment at current sweep x. But sweep x changes; store current_x in a mutable holder or recompute keys lazily. Since bisect with key calls key(element) and compares to x\'s key... We\'d call bisect with x being a sentinel segment and key = lambda seg: y_of(seg, current_sweep_x). current_sweep_x stored in module-level/state variable updated before each bisect. That works and matches "a list with bisect against a comparator function is acceptable". I\'ll write my own bisect over comparator to be explicit, or use bisect with key. Using stdlib bisect with key= is cleanest; document complexity: insertions/deletions O(n) due to list, lookups O(log n) comparisons each O(1)-ish (Fraction arithmetic though—each y-at-x evaluation is O(log?) big-int ops; treat as O(1) word ops honestly? Just say O(log n) comparisons per operation, list insert/delete O(n), so worst case O(n² ) overall for the status structure — honest documentation).\n\nEvent handling (with no vertical segments, no triple points, but endpoint degeneracies allowed):\n\nAt event point p with x = p.x:\n1. Pop all events equal to p (same (x,y)) from queue.\n2. Determine segments ending at p, starting at p, and crossing at p (interior-interior crossing). With exact arithmetic and general-ish position (no triples), at most... crossing at interior point of both segments happens only at their mutual intersection; can\'t have more than one pair crossing at p (no triples). But p could be: \n   - start of A, end of B (endpoint touch)\n   - start of A, interior of B\n   - interior of A, end of B\n   - interior crossing of A and B (both interiors)\n   - end of both A and B\n   - start of both A and B\n   - start of A only, etc.\n\nStandard approach handling all cases: at event p:\n   - L = segments with right(endpoint at p... wait orientation) Let me define: left endpoint = smaller x (since non-vertical, x strictly increases along segment). Events: left endpoint, right endpoint, intersection.\n\n   At sweep position p:\n   - RL: segments for which p is the right endpoint.\n   - LR: segments for which p is the left endpoint.\n   - C: segments containing p in their interior (these are in the status structure, found by locate).\n\n   Remove RL ∪ C from status, swap order appropriately, reinsert RL∪LR... The classic: remove all segments ending or crossing at p; find neighbors pred/succ around removal gap; reinsert crossing + left-endpoint segments; check new adjacencies for intersections; also check adjacency between pred-of-gap and succ-of-gap after removal (only needed if nothing reinserted).\n\nSince no triple concurrency, at most 2 segments involved at p. That simplifies enormously! Cases:\n   - 0 segments (shouldn\'t happen except... every event belongs to ≥1 segment; intersection events belong to 2).\n   - 1 segment: left endpoint or right endpoint of a single segment. After processing, check new adjacency for future intersection.\n   - 2 segments: various combos.\n\nSimple robust implementation: at event p, collect all segments incident to p (from event data + status lookup). Remove from status all segments that end at p or pass through p. Determine insertion set: segments starting at p (insert), segments passing through p (reinsert — their relative order may swap after crossing; with ≤2 involved, just reinsert them fresh). Then check all adjacent pairs among {pred, newly inserted block, succ} for intersections strictly to the right of p (or at... intersections at x>p; intersections at x==p already handled since we process events in order and only enqueue future intersections with x>p, or x==p but y>p? No—non-vertical means same x implies different points along different segments... two segments crossing at x==p.x but y>p.y? Both contain points with x=p.x; if they intersect at some q with q.x == p.x and q ≠ p, impossible since a non-vertical segment has at most one point per x. So future intersections have x>p, OR x==p impossible. Wait, but when we\'re at event p and check adjacency of segments S1,S2, their intersection q satisfies q.x > p.x always? q could equal p only if they also meet at p — excluded (≤2 segments at p, if S1,S2 both at p they\'re the pair being handled; after reinsertion we shouldn\'t re-report). When checking adjacency after inserting at p, candidate intersections must be filtered: q.x > p.x, or (q.x == p.x — impossible as argued). Also q must not be behind. Also filter q == p duplicates.\n\nStandard correctness detail: when computing intersection of adjacent S1,S2, if intersection exists and q.x > p.x, add event. Also handle q == p (already processed) — skip.\n\nOrdering within event processing matters: process all events at the same (x,y) together.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass _State:\n    ...\n```\n\nLet me write the sweep concretely.\n\nSegments given as list of ((x1,y1),(x2,y2)) with integer coordinates (or Fraction-convertible). Validate non-vertical.\n\nPreprocess: ensure left endpoint first (sort by x).\n\nEvent queue entries: (x, y, kind, data) where kind in {\'L\',\'R\',\'X\'}? For heap ordering we need deterministic tie-breaks: (x, y, kind_order, seg_index...) — but all events at same (x,y) get popped together anyway, so tie-break just needs stability, not semantic order. Use (x, y, seq) with seq counter to avoid comparing dicts.\n\nProcessing loop:\n\n```\nwhile heap:\n    p = pop all events with same (x,y)\n    handle_event(p, associated)\n```\n\nhandle_event:\n- ends = set of seg indices with R-event at p\n- starts = set of seg indices with L-events at p\n- crosses = segments in status containing p in interior: find via status search: locate position(s) where y_at(p.x) == p.y. Since ≤2 segments at p total (validated), crosses = the pair from validation? We know from validation exactly which point pairs intersect where. Hmm, but the sweep shouldn\'t rely on validation results conceptually... it\'s fine practically, but nicer for the sweep to discover crossings itself via adjacency checks — that\'s the whole point of BO. Interior crossings get discovered when segments become adjacent. So at event p, crosses are discovered because when we removed/reinserted neighbors earlier we enqueued X events. At event time, X events tell us which segments cross at p. So:\n\nEvent kinds: (\'L\', seg_idx), (\'R\', seg_idx), (\'X\', idx_i, idx_j).\n\nhandle p:\n- Gather: R_set from popped R events; X_pairs from popped X events; L_set from popped L events.\n- crossing segments C = union of segments in X_pairs (each X pair has exactly 2 distinct segments; no triples so pairs at p share no segments across pairs... actually with ≤2 segments at p, there\'s at most ONE X pair at p).\n- Remove R_set ∪ C from status. Before removal record pred/succ boundaries: when removing a contiguous block, the elements just below and just above the block. Simplest: remove all of R_set ∪ C individually, then reinsert starts ∪ (C - ... wait C segments pass through p, they must be reinserted (they continue to the right). Segments in R_set end. Insert L_set.\n- After all removals and before insertions, note neighbors: lo_pred = element just below removal region, hi_succ = just above. Easiest robust approach:\n  1. remove R_set ∪ C from status.\n  2. candidates for new adjacency checks: after inserting L_set ∪ C back, check adjacency pairs formed around inserted items, plus if nothing was inserted, check (pred, succ) that became adjacent after removal.\n  \nWith tiny local sets this is easy: implement helper `check_pair(i, j)` that enqueues intersection if valid (q strictly right of p, i.e., q.x > p.x or q.x==p.x impossible; and q not equal to previously... just q.x>p.x suffices? If two segments cross at q with q.x > p.x fine. If they touch at q with q.x == p.x — impossible as shown. If q == p they were the X pair already handled.)\n\nHmm wait, one more: after inserting, need to check pairs (new_item, pred), (new_item, succ), and among multiple inserted items (at most... L_set could have many segments starting at same point! Multiple segments sharing a left endpoint at p — allowed (endpoint touching, only 3-segment *concurrent intersection* rejected... wait, two segments sharing an endpoint IS a 2-segment concurrency, fine. Three segments sharing an endpoint = triple concurrency → rejected by validation. So at most 2 segments start at p.) OK so L_set size ≤2, C size ≤2 combined total segments at p ≤2. Nice invariant: |R| + |L| + |C| ≤ 2.\n\nGiven ≤2 segments at p, the logic is very tractable:\n\nCases:\n- Single segment s (L or R only):\n  - If L: insert s into status; check adjacency (pred, s) and (s, succ).\n  - If R: remove s; check (pred, succ) adjacency.\n- Two segments a,b:\n  Sub-cases by roles:\n  - Both L (shared left endpoint at p): insert both in correct y-order; check (pred,a),(a,b),(b,succ).\n  - Both R (shared right endpoint): remove both; check (pred,succ).\n  - a R, b L (endpoint chain touch): remove a, insert b; check (pred,b),(b,succ) and also... after removing a, pred-succ gap gets b inserted; also should we check (pred, ...) hmm: removal of a makes its pred and succ adjacent, but then b is inserted somewhere near. Checking (pred,b),(b,succ) covers pairs involving b; pair (pred_of_a, succ_of_a) without b matters only if neither pred nor succ is b... e.g., a removed, b inserted at a different y-position, now old pred and old succ of a become adjacent to each other possibly with b between them? If b is between them, pairs (pred,b),(b,succ) cover it. If b is outside [pred,succ] range... can b be outside? b starts at p, its y-at-p.x is p.y; a ends at p, y-at-p.x = p.y. All segments containing p have y_at(p.x)==p.y, so they\'re adjacent-ish in status at x slightly right of p: b continues rightward, a doesn\'t exist right of p. pred/succ of a are below/above p.y level. b inserts exactly where a was (roughly, modulo ties at same y? another segment could have y_at(p.x) == p.y without containing p? No—y_at(p.x)==p.y means it contains point (p.x,p.y)... wait it contains the point on its own line at x=p.x, which is (p.x, y). If y == p.y then that point IS p. But that would mean a third segment passes through p! Excluded. Unless the segment\'s endpoint... no: any segment with y_at(p.x) == p.y contains the point (p.x, p.y) = p. So exactly the ≤2 segments at p have that value.) Great: b occupies exactly a\'s position in status. So check (pred_a, b) and (b, succ_a). Covered by generic "after modifications, check adjacencies around changed region".\n  - a and b cross at p (X event, both interior): remove both, reinsert swapped; check (pred, lower_new), (upper_new, succ). (The pair (a,b) themselves just crossed; no need to re-check each other.)\n  - a L (starts at p), b interior crossing?? b crosses at p means X event with a — but a starts at p, b interior contains p → they intersect at p, discovered how? When a was inserted... a starts at p, so at insertion time we check adjacency: b is pred or succ, check_pair(a,b) finds q=p? Filter q.x>p.x would skip it! Problem: intersection AT the start endpoint of a. Hmm. So check_pair needs care: intersections at p itself are handled at p\'s event, but discovery timing varies. Solution: allow enqueueing events with q >= p in the order (q.x > p.x) or (q.x == p.x) — but q.x == p.x and q.y vs p.y? q == p necessarily (single point per x per segment; both contain p... a contains p (its left endpoint), b contains p → intersection point is p itself if they intersect only there — but wait, if b passes through p in its interior and a starts at p, could a and b ALSO intersect elsewhere? They\'d be collinear-overlapping — rejected. So intersection is exactly {p}.) So when check_pair(a,b) at event p discovers q == p, we must report p as an intersection NOW (add to results), not enqueue. But then the X handling at p: our event processing at p must know a and b meet at p. Timing problem: a\'s L-event at p is being processed now; b interior. We discover intersection at p during this event\'s processing. So handle: when check_pair finds q == current p, immediately mark both as "crossing at p": i.e., treat like an X at p: both continue; need to swap their order? Do they need swapping in status? a starts at p, b passes through p. Immediately to the right of p, their order is determined by directions. Before p, a wasn\'t present, b was. Insert a at correct position for right-of-p order. If we insert a using y_at(p.x) both equal p.y — tie! Tie broken arbitrarily could be wrong for right-side ordering. Hmm.\n\nThis is the classic subtlety. Robust fix: evaluate status order at x = p.x + ε conceptually — i.e., compare segments by y at a point slightly RIGHT of the event x. Exact way: comparator compares y_i(x\') vs y_j(x\') where x\' = next "interesting" x? Standard trick: since no vertical segments, order of two segments can only change at their intersection; compare by y at sweep x using exact fractions, breaking ties (equal y at x, meaning they meet at x) by slopes: the one with... at a meeting point, right-order is determined by slope: segment with larger slope is above to the right? y_right ≈ p.y + slope*dx; larger slope → higher on the right. So tie-break by slope ascending (larger slope = higher = later in bottom-to-top order).\n\nSo define key/compare(seg) at current event x: primary y_at(x) as Fraction, secondary slope (dy/dx as Fraction). This handles all ties correctly for the right-side ordering, including shared endpoints. And removal: find segment by identity (index), not by key — use a dict or list.remove. Status stores segment indices; comparator uses current_x and precomputed slopes.\n\nBut careful: using tie-break-by-slope, when processing event p, we set current_x = p.x, then all segments through p tie in y and sort by slope. Removals by identity. Insertions land correctly. Adjacency neighbor checks then correct.\n\nOne more subtlety: X event at p for a,b both interior: we must swap their order. If we just remove both and reinsert with current_x = p.x, comparator puts them by slope — automatically correct right-side order. \n\nAnd L event with intersection at left endpoint (discovered via check_pair returning q == p): report p, and insertion order by slope is already right; nothing else needed. Similarly R event discovering q==p? R at p: segment ends; its intersection with neighbor at p would mean neighbor also contains p — that\'s the 2-segment endpoint-touch case: a ends at p, b... if b\'s endpoint at p too (R,R) or b starts at p (R,L) or b passes through p (interior). Discovery paths: (R,R): when the second of them became adjacent... they\'ve been adjacent since whichever came later; check_pair at that earlier time found q=p (their shared endpoint) with q.x > then-current-x → enqueued X event at p. Good, arrives as X. Then processing: R events for both at p (we pop R events at p) plus X event at p. Consistent. (R,L): a ends at p, b starts at p. a\'s R-event processed at p; b\'s L-event also at p (popped together). check_pair during processing discovers q==p → report. But careful not to double-report: report set semantics — we collect results in a SET of points, so duplicates harmless. \n\n(R + interior b): b interior at p, a ends at p. Discovered earlier when a,b became adjacent → X event enqueued at p. Processing pops X(a,b) and R(a). Fine: C={b}? X says a,b cross at p, but a ends at p — treat: remove both (a ends anyway, b continues, reinsert b). Generic code: R_set={a}, C from X={a,b}, union remove {a,b}, reinsert C\\R... reinsert segments that continue past p: b. Correct!\n\n(L + interior b): as discussed, discovered at L-processing via check_pair q==p, report immediately. But also possible: X event for (a,b) at p was ALREADY enqueued?? b interior crossing... a starts at p; before p, a didn\'t exist, so adjacency (a,b) never checked before p; X can\'t be pre-enqueued. OK.\n\nBut wait — there\'s still a subtle miss: check_pair(a, b) called during event p where q==p: report p. Also if q < p?? Can\'t be: adjacency established at some event ≤ p; when adjacency formed at event p\' we checked pairs with filter q.x > p\'.x (or q==p\' handled). Later adjacency unchanged until next event... adjacency between a,b persists until one of them hits an event. Events processed in order; if check at p\' enqueued q>p\', then at any later event p\'\'>p\' before q, no recheck needed. Within event p processing, after structural changes, new adjacencies checked with q.x > p.x or q == p (report now). Could check_pair at p find q with p.x < ... q.x == p.x but q != p? Impossible (shown). Could it find q.x < p.x? Two segments both existing right of p... their intersection left of p would have been found earlier — but with tie-break-slope ordering, could a stale adjacency pair have intersection left of current p? If a,b adjacent at time p and intersect at q with q.x < p.x, then they crossed at q; at q they\'d both be present... unless one started between q and p? If a starts at p_L ∈ (q.x, p.x)... a starts at p_L, intersection q.x < p_L.x impossible since a only exists right of p_L, q on a → q.x ≥ p_L.x > q.x contradiction. If both existed before q, they were adjacent-or-not at q time... if intersecting at q they must have been adjacent around q (order swaps there), checked at event ≤ q. Edge: they crossed at q but another segment passed exactly through q → triple, rejected. Or they touched at q tangentially... two straight segments touching without crossing: only at endpoints (T-touch or V-touch), handled by endpoint logic. Interior-interior intersection = crossing. I\'m fairly confident: with the slope tie-break and reporting q==p immediately, filter "enqueue iff q.x > p.x, elif q == p report, else ignore" is sound.\n\nHmm, one more classic pitfall: reporting q==p when q is the shared point of a,b where BOTH are being removed at p (R,R case arriving via X) — double counting avoided by set. And X event arriving at p where the pair also has L/R roles — generic union handles.\n\nWhat about an X event whose q.x > current event x (stale)? Can\'t: heap pops in order, and when enqueued q.x > current. Events at same x different y: fine, order by y — but does processing order at same x matter? Intersections at same x but different y on same segment: segment\'s y-at-x unique, events independent. Processing order among same-x events: we pop ALL same-(x,y) together, but different-y same-x events processed sequentially with current_x = that event\'s x — status comparator uses current_x = p.x for all these; correct since ordering by y-at-x valid for any x in the strip between event xs... at exactly x = p.x with slope tie-break, consistent. Fine.\n\nStale X events (pair no longer adjacent when popped): possible? Classic implementations guard. With exact arithmetic and complete neighbor checks, spurious/stale events can occur if a pair\'s intersection was enqueued, then one of the pair gets removed (ended) or they stop being adjacent due to an intervening... can they stop being adjacent without one being removed? Adjacency changes only at events involving one of them (insert/remove/cross-swap). Cross-swap keeps adjacency. If segment c is inserted between a and b — possible if c\'s y-at-x falls between them while a,b still adjacent pair with pending event q? If q = a∩b and c passes between them before q... c between a,b at current x, but a,b meet at q: for c to slip between, at some x0 < q.x, then at x0 event... c insertion at x0 triggers checks (c,pred),(c,succ) — a,b not adjacent anymore, but do we lose q? When c inserted between, we check (a,c) and (c,b) — their intersections enqueued. Original q=a∩b event remains in heap; when popped, a,b not adjacent anymore — q would still be a genuine intersection point! Wait, a and b DO intersect at q regardless of adjacency. The event q is real; when popped we process it as crossing: remove both, reinsert swapped, check neighbors. Is that harmful? a,b crossing at q: at q, both contain q; if c also passed through q → triple (rejected). Otherwise processing X(a,b) at q is correct even if not adjacent. But danger: DOUBLE processing if q got enqueued twice? Enqueue dedup: keep a set of pending (frozenset{a,b}) → skip duplicates. Points set dedups output anyway; but structural double-processing (remove twice, reinsert twice) corrupts status. Guard: when popping X(a,b), verify both still in status; also dedup via pending set. Also X popped where one already ended (removed): skip if not in status. Can a genuinely-crossing pair have one removed before q? a ends at r with r.x < q.x — but q on a needs q.x ≤ r.x. Contradiction, safe. So staleness only from... fine, guard anyway.\n\nAlso: when X(a,b) processed and a or b also has R/L role at q — union handles.\n\nAlso dedup L/R events naturally unique per segment. Pending-set for X: `pending = set()` of frozensets; on pop, discard.\n\nReport: results set of (Fraction,Fraction) points. Report at: X events (point q), and check_pair discoveries q==p during processing. Endpoint-touch intersections: (R,R) via X, (R,L) & (L,L-shared-endpoint) via check_pair q==p, (L, interior-b) via check_pair q==p. Also (interior a, R b)?? b ends at p, a interior: symmetric to (L, interior) — discovered when? a,b adjacent since before; check_pair at earlier event enqueued q=p_earlier? q=p has p.x > earlier.x → X enqueued at p. Popped with R(b) and X(a,b): C={a,b}\\R → reinsert a. Good.\n\nEdge: segment of zero length (p1==p2)? Reject? Not mentioned; a zero-length "segment"... I\'ll reject with ValueError probably. Tests generate distinct endpoints presumably; add validation raising ValueError for zero-length, vertical → NotImplementedError per spec. Zero-length: treat as invalid input ValueError. Keep simple: require x1 != x2 (vertical → NotImplementedError), and (x1,y1)!=(x2,y2) else ValueError. Actually a zero-length segment with distinct x? No—if endpoints equal, dx=0 → caught by vertical check first. Order: check p1==p2 → ValueError("degenerate"), check x1==x2 → NotImplementedError.\n\nComparator details: for segment i with left endpoint l_i, right r_i: y_at(i, x) = l_y + (r_y - l_y) * (x - l_x)/(r_x - l_x) as Fraction. Slope = (r_y-l_y)/(r_x-l_x). Compare key(i) = (y_at, slope). Precompute slope Fractions per segment once. y_at computed on demand.\n\nStatus ops:\n- `insert(idx)`: bisect by comparator key → insort.\n- `remove(idx)`: list.remove(idx) O(n).\n- neighbors of a contiguous block: find index of one member via list.index O(n), expand.\n\nSince ≤2 segments per event, code straightforward.\n\ncheck_pair(i,j): compute exact intersection of segments i,j:\n- d = cross(r_i - l_i, r_j - l_j) (direction vectors). If 0 → collinear lines; overlap rejected in validation, but collinear TOUCHING at one point possible: if d==0, intersection point exists only if they share an endpoint point q (collinear touch). Compute: q candidates = shared endpoints. Check membership. If q found: handle like normal q (report if q==p, enqueue if q.x>p.x). Collinear touch where q is endpoint of one and endpoint of other, or endpoint of one lying ON the other\'s interior — collinear: endpoint of A in interior of B, e.g., A=(0,0)-(2,0), B=(-1,0)-(5,0) — overlap! (A ⊂ B) → rejected. Non-overlap collinear touch must be end-to-end: q is endpoint of both. But wait validation rejects overlap; end-to-end touch survives. However during sweep check_pair could see collinear pair only if validation missed... validation catches all overlaps; end-to-end touch pairs: d==0, q = shared endpoint. Handle: if d==0: find common endpoint; if none, no intersection (can\'t happen for adjacent-in-status collinear segments? whatever, return None). Treat q normally.\n- d != 0: t = cross(l_j - l_i, d_j)/d, s = cross(l_j - l_i, d_i)/d... let me derive: point = l_i + t*d_i = l_j + s*d_j. Solve: t = cross(l_j - l_i, d_j)/cross(d_i,d_j), s = cross(l_j - l_i, d_i)/cross(d_i,d_j). Intersection within segments iff 0≤t≤1 and 0≤s≤1. q = (l_i.x + t*d_i.x, l_i.y + t*d_i.y) Fractions. Return q or None.\n\nThen in check_pair: if q None → nothing. If q == p (Fractions equal): report p... but ONLY if the pair actually meets at p — yes q==p means that. Report. Note: for (a,b) where a is being inserted at p and b interior — report p. But careful: q==p when p is, e.g., a\'s left endpoint and b\'s left endpoint — L,L shared endpoint: check_pair(a,b) at insertion finds q==p, report. Good. Also make sure we don\'t enqueue X at p itself (would cause weird reprocessing): rule — enqueue only q.x > p.x; q==p → report directly into results set.\n\nHmm wait, subtle: q == p but the X event for this pair at p might ALSO arrive via heap (e.g., R,R case: enqueued earlier as X at p, and at p we pop X(a,b) AND R(a), R(b)). Processing X reports q; fine, set dedup. But also during that same event\'s check_pair calls (neighbors), could re-discover q==p → report again → set dedup. OK.\n\nDouble-processing risk for X(a,b) enqueued twice: pending set prevents. Also X(a,b) where at pop time a or b missing from status → skip. When could that legitimately happen... (R,R): both removed at p — but we pop X together WITH the R events at p (same (x,y) batch), so they\'re still in status when X handled if we process X before removals. Batch processing: gather all events at p FIRST, then handle jointly. Order inside: compute R_set, L_set, X_pairs. Then: remove R_set ∪ X_segments, reinsert X_segments minus R_set plus L_set. So X handled structurally in joint step; stale check unnecessary within batch. Stale = X pair where one segment already removed at EARLIER event — argued impossible. Bu