un among leaving∩... general rule: check ALL consecutive pairs in the sequence [pred] + leaving_block + inserted + [succ]? The leaving block members are mutually adjacent-ish at p (contiguous tie group). Any pair within leaving block that intersect at p: e.g., (R,R) shared endpoint — but that arrives via X anyway... does it? (R,R) shared endpoint p: when did a,b become adjacent? Earlier event; check_pair enqueued q=p (q.x>earlier.x) ✓ X arrives. So (R,R) covered. But chain case (R(a),L(b)) collinear: pair (a,b) both in "leaving ∪ inserted" sequence adjacency — a∈leaving, b∈inserted, contiguous in the conceptual merged sequence. So: build merged sequence M = [pred] + sorted(leaving, key) + sorted(inserted, key) + [succ] and check consecutive pairs of M. Redundant checks harmless (dedup via pending/set). Does that create issues? check_pair(a,b) with a removed already — fine, pure predicate. q==p → report ✓. \n\nBut careful about ordering within merged: leaving and inserted both tie-group; merge them all sorted by key — same keys possible (slope equal, collinear!) — collinear pair a,b same slope same y: order arbitrary; consecutive ✓ checked either way.\n\nRedo (R,L) non-collinear: M=[pred]+[a]+[b]+[succ] (or b,a order by slope); checks include (a,b): q==p report — correct! Chain touch IS an intersection (a\'s end = b\'s start) ✓. Previously covered via (pred,b)? No—but now covered properly. Also non-collinear chain previously: did old scheme catch it? (R(a),L(b)): checks were (pred,b),(b,succ) — (a,b) missed unless X pre-enqueued: was it? a,b adjacent before p? a\'s pred could be b if b... b not yet inserted before p (starts at p). So missed! My earlier analysis was WRONG — good thing merged-M fixes it uniformly. Let me re-examine: non-collinear chain a ends at p, b starts at p: intersection at p must be reported. M-scheme: (a,b) consecutive in M ⇒ checked ⇒ q==p ⇒ report ✓✓.\n\nSimilarly (L,L) shared endpoint: both in inserted; consecutive in inserted-block ⇒ checked